        {# ── Authentication ──────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
        <section id="auth" class="mb-5">
            <h2 class="h5 border-bottom pb-2 mb-3">Authentication</h2>
            <p>All API endpoints require a token. Tokens are created and managed on the <a href="{{ path('api_token_index') }}">API Tokens</a> page. Two header formats are supported:</p>

            <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-1">Bearer token <span class="text-muted fw-normal">(preferred)</span></h3>
            <pre class="bg-dark text-light rounded p-3 small"><code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;your-token&gt;

# Example with curl:
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;your-token&gt;" https://example.com/api/...</code></pre>

            <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-1">HTTP Basic Auth</h3>
            <p class="small">For clients that only support HTTP Basic Auth, send the token as the <strong>password</strong>. The username field is ignored.</p>
            <pre class="bg-dark text-light rounded p-3 small"><code>Authorization: Basic &lt;base64(anyuser:your-token)&gt;

# Example with curl:
curl -u "token:&lt;your-token&gt;" https://example.com/api/...</code></pre>

            <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-1">IP / CIDR restrictions</h3>
            <p class="small mb-0">Every general-purpose token must be restricted to one or more source IP addresses or CIDR ranges (IPv4 and IPv6 both supported). Requests from addresses outside the allowlist are rejected with <code>403</code>. Restrictions are configured when creating or editing a token.</p>

            <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-1">Expiry</h3>
            <p class="small mb-0">Every general-purpose token must have an expiry date. Requests made with an expired token are rejected with <code>401</code>. The default expiry when creating a token is five years from the creation date. Tokens can be extended by editing them.</p>

            <h3 class="h6 mt-3 mb-1">Host-scoped tokens</h3>
            <p class="small">A separate token type used exclusively with the <a href="#host-self">Host Self-Service</a> endpoints. Host-scoped tokens are tied to a specific host and carry no IP allowlist or expiry — instead, access is automatically restricted to the IP addresses assigned to the host's network interfaces. Requests from any other IP are rejected with <code>401</code>. These tokens are generated from the host detail page and can only access <code>/api/self/*</code> routes. They are intended for ACME DNS-01 certificate automation running directly on the host.</p>
            <p class="small mb-0">Use the same Bearer or Basic auth header format shown above. A host can have at most one active token; generating a new one immediately revokes the previous.</p>

            <p class="mt-3 mb-0 text-muted small">Always use <strong>HTTPS</strong>. HTTP requests will be redirected and the Authorization header will be stripped by the redirect.</p>
        </section>

